LCOS 1.1 boot menu
===================

UEFI
----
rEFInd (the Devuan refind package, 0.14.2) is the picker. The LCOS tile
chainloads the GRUB already installed on the EFI system partition
(EFI/devuan or EFI/debian). GRUB still asks for the disk passphrase when
the install is encrypted, still sets $lcos_quirks from SMBIOS (Dell XPS
13 SKU 0E53), and still picks up kernels from update-grub.

The GRUB menu is hidden (timeout 0) so there are not two menus. To open
it: highlight LCOS, press Insert, F2 or +, and choose "Boot options".
Holding Shift as GRUB starts does the same. That menu has older kernels
and anything os-prober found.

rEFInd scans Windows (EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi), Haiku
(EFI/Haiku/haiku_loader.efi), other Linux GRUB directories, and macOS
(System/Library/CoreServices/boot.efi on HFS+). It does not scan
/boot for vmlinuz, and the ext4/btrfs drivers are not installed, so it
cannot boot a kernel directly and skip GRUB.

Last choice is stored in EFI/refind/refind-vars (use_nvram false), not
in firmware NVRAM. Timeout is 5 seconds. Mouse is on. The second row is
shutdown, reboot, and firmware setup.

If EFI/BOOT/bootx64.efi is a byte-for-byte copy of this install's GRUB,
install replaces it with rEFInd plus the theme, so a firmware that
ignores NVRAM still opens the picker. Any other bootx64.efi is left
alone.

Secure Boot is unsupported. Do not enroll a MOK; the kernel is unsigned.
The refind package's debconf question "install to the ESP" is forced off
by /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/90lcos-refind-no-esp so installing or upgrading
refind does not write the boot partition by itself.

BIOS
----
There is no rEFInd. GRUB uses /usr/share/grub/themes/lcos (navy field,
the smooth badge, DejaVu, icons for Linux, Windows, macOS and Haiku).
Timeout stays at 5 seconds and os-prober still lists other systems.

Upgrades from 1.0.x
-------------------
Installing this package does not modify the EFI system partition and
does not run update-grub. The theme drop-in applies the next time
grub-mkconfig runs (a later kernel update). To switch a UEFI machine
that is already installed:

    sudo lcos-bootmenu install

To go back to a visible GRUB menu and remove the rEFInd files this
package wrote:

    sudo lcos-bootmenu remove

`refresh` is the same as `install`.

What rEFInd cannot do
---------------------
Labels under auto-detected loaders are rEFInd's own sentences
("Boot Microsoft EFI boot from …"), so the theme leaves them visible
rather than inventing a second menu. 32-bit UEFI (early Intel Macs)
keeps GRUB: the amd64 refind package has no refind_ia32.efi. APFS
macOS needs a driver Devuan does not ship; HFS+ Intel Mac volumes are
scanned. Shutdown uses rEFInd's firmware call, which reboots some
UEFI machines instead of powering off. A firmware that ignores the
boot variables and only starts EFI/BOOT/bootx64.efi gets rEFInd only
when that file was a copy of our GRUB; a Windows-owned fallback is
not replaced.
